    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body": "### Contributing guidelines\r\n\r\n- [X] I have read [CONTRIBUTING.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md)\r\n- [X] I have read [CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md)\r\n\r\n### Module(s)\r\n\r\nmini.operators / multiply operator\r\n\r\n### Description\r\n\r\nFirst, thanks again for this plugin. As I mentioned on reddit, a multiplication operator is quite transformative for the vim experience, and I wouldn't want to use nvim without it.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nSo I noticed with linewise multiplication operations, the cursor always moved to the first non-whitespace character of the line.\r\n\r\n```lua\r\n-- current behavior\r\n--                    v    cursor position before multiplication via `gmm`\r\n   local foobar = \"baz\"\r\n   local foobar = \"baz\"\r\n-- ^                       cursor position after\r\n\r\n-- expected/desired behavior\r\n   local foobar = \"baz\"\r\n--                    ^    cursor position after\r\n```\r\n\r\nHowever, it is often quite desirable to keep the cursor in the same column as before the multiplication. A real use case where that matters are for instance lua annotations: When I want to add a field by duplicating the line with `@field`, it always moves me to the beginning of the line, instead of staying on `field_name`.\r\n\r\n```lua\r\n---@class (exact) class_name\r\n---@field field_name string\r\n```\r\n\r\nCould this behavior of the multiply operator be changed? (either by default or as an option, not sure which makes more sense)",
